  <content>
  <section><title>Writing Equations Using Symbols</title>

<!-- <p> When writing statements using algebraic symbols, the following steps may be helpful. </p> -->

<note>
  <para><emphasis>How to write statements using algebraic symbols</emphasis></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Read the problem. <emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Identify the variables and known values. If needed, sketch a picture of the scenario.<emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write a sentence using the relationship among the values. <emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Translate the sentence into an equation.</para>
</note>
<para>Remember that <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">variables</term> represent values in the problem that can change.</para>


  <para>Let's examine two examples of how to write statements algebraically using symbols.</para>


<para><emphasis>Example 1</emphasis></para>
 <para>We want to express the following statement using symbolic language: <span>The sum </span><span>of three consecutive integers is 372.</span></para>

<para> <emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis> - Read the problem. </para>

<para><emphasis>Step 2 </emphasis> - Identify the variables and known values. If needed, sketch a picture of the scenario.</para>

<para> Let \( x \) = the first integer. There are two more numbers that follow \( x \). </para>

<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dde7b58e23c5555930c3d2a06a33cfe3418f9f5f"/></media></figure><para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write a sentence using the relationship among the values. </para>

<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Translate the sentence into an equation. </para>

<para><emphasis>Example 2</emphasis></para>
 <para>We want to express the following statement using symbolic language: <span>of three consecutive odd integers is 93.</span></para>
 
 <para> <emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis> - Read the problem. </para>
 
 <para><emphasis>Step 2 </emphasis> - Identify the variables and known values. If needed, sketch a picture of the scenario.</para>
 
 <para> Let \(x\) = the first integer. There are two more ODD numbers that follow \(x\). </para>
 
 <figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/253d151822d1b5afe49f1825ecf98d34e5b4768d"/></media></figure><para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write a sentence using the relationship among the values. </para>
 
 <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Translate the sentence into an equation. </para>

 <title>Try It: Writing Equations Using Symbols</title>
<para><span>Now it’s your turn. Write the statement below algebraically using symbols.</span></para>
<para>The sum of three consecutive integers is 1,623.</para>
<para><span>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</span></para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
  </problem><solution>
  <para><span>Here is how to express this statement using symbols.</span></para>
  <para> \( x+(x+1)+(x+2)=1,623 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
</example></section>
